## 7. PLAYER MOVEMENT AND PHYSICS

Implement a reliable player controller using Phaser 3 physics.

Required functionality:
- Move left and right.
- Smooth acceleration and deceleration where appropriate.
- Gravity.
- Jumping.
- Reliable ground detection.
- Falling detection.
- Collision with solid platforms.
- Damage feedback.
- Temporary invulnerability after damage.
- Death and respawning.
- Checkpoint respawning.
- Camera tracking.

Use a simple health system with three hearts or three lives.

A single jump input must produce one jump. Prevent infinite jumping, unintended double jumps, and jumping repeatedly while airborne.

Allow a jump only when the player is grounded.

Variable-height jumping is optional. If it makes the controls less reliable, use a simple fixed-height jump.

Use a separate physics body when necessary so transparent image margins do not affect collisions.

The character should never walk through solid platforms or become permanently stuck in terrain.

When the player falls into a pit or loses all health, handle the situation cleanly.

After taking damage, provide brief visual feedback without excessive flashing or screen shake.

### Animation support

Support these states when matching graphics are available:
- Idle.
- Running.
- Jumping.
- Falling.
- Taking damage.
- Death.

If only a static player image is available, display it correctly and keep the game playable.

Do not require every animation asset before the game can start.

